window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["gcp"]=[{"id":"gcp-f0001","card":"q-gcp-001-1-intro-and-core-ideas","sec":"gcp-s002","q":"What is traffic splitting in Google App Engine, and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>It sends a chosen percentage of requests to different versions of the same service at the same time. It solves risky all-at-once releases: you can canary a new version on a few percent of users, run an A/B test, or move to a new version and go back with no downtime.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0002","card":"q-gcp-001-1-intro-and-core-ideas","sec":"gcp-s002","q":"In Google Cloud Storage, what is object lifecycle management and why would you use it?","a":"<p>It is a set of bucket rules that automatically delete objects or move them to a cheaper storage class when conditions are met (age, storage class, number of newer versions). It cuts storage cost and removes manual cleanup scripts, for example moving logs to Archive after 30 days and deleting them after a year.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0003","card":"q-gcp-001-1-intro-and-core-ideas","sec":"gcp-s002","q":"How can Cloud Tasks together with App Engine help you handle a large batch workload?","a":"<p>Cloud Tasks turns the work into many small HTTP tasks and dispatches them at a controlled rate. App Engine then autoscales instances to process those requests in parallel, and scales back down when the queue is empty. You do not run one long request that times out.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0004","card":"q-gcp-002-project-setup","sec":"gcp-s003","q":"In Google Cloud, what is the difference between a project name, a project ID and a project number?","a":"<p>The name is a human label and can be changed. The project ID is globally unique, chosen at creation and cannot be changed; it appears in URLs, resource names and config. The project number is assigned by Google and is used internally, for example in some service account names. Use the ID in scripts and <code>gcloud</code> commands.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0005","card":"q-gcp-002-project-setup","sec":"gcp-s003","q":"Why does a Google Cloud project need a billing account, and what does the project act as?","a":"<p>A project is the container that owns resources, IAM policies, enabled APIs and quotas. Paid resources only work when the project is linked to a billing account, which receives the charges. Different projects (for example test and production) can share a billing account yet stay isolated from each other.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0006","card":"q-gcp-002-project-setup","sec":"gcp-s003","q":"Where does a project sit in the Google Cloud resource hierarchy?","a":"<p>Organization, then folders, then projects, then resources. IAM policies and organization policies set at a higher level are inherited downward. That is why teams group projects in folders to apply shared access rules.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0007","card":"q-gcp-003-console-dashboard-cards","sec":"gcp-s004","q":"How do you move between organizations, folders and projects in the Google Cloud Console, and where do you open IAM from?","a":"<p>Use the resource (project) switcher in the top bar. It also leads to IAM and organization policies. The search bar finds projects, products and items inside products.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0008","card":"q-gcp-004-cloud-shell","sec":"gcp-s005","q":"What is Cloud Shell, and when is it better than installing the Cloud SDK locally?","a":"<p>It is a free, browser-based Linux terminal running on a Google-managed VM with <code>gcloud</code>, language runtimes, Docker and an editor preinstalled and already authenticated to your project. It is better for quick tasks, for a locked-down laptop, or for a clean tool setup. Install the SDK locally for long-running work, IDE integration and scripts that need your own environment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0009","card":"q-gcp-004-cloud-shell","sec":"gcp-s005","q":"What persists in Cloud Shell between sessions, and what does not?","a":"<p>Your home directory (about 5 GB of persistent disk) persists. The VM itself is temporary and is recycled after inactivity, so anything installed outside the home directory is lost. Keep scripts and dotfiles in the home directory. Limits and specs can change, so check the current documentation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0010","card":"q-gcp-004-cloud-shell","sec":"gcp-s005","q":"In Cloud Shell, how do you view a web server you started on port 8080 in your browser?","a":"<p>Use Web Preview, then Preview on port 8080 (or Change port for another port). Cloud Shell proxies a temporary, authenticated URL to the process listening in the VM, and only you can open it. A quick test is <code>python3 -m http.server 8080</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0011","card":"q-gcp-005-installing-and-initializing-the-cloud-sd","sec":"gcp-s006","q":"What does <code>gcloud init</code> do, and what do you choose during it?","a":"<p>It sets up the first named configuration for the CLI. You log in through the browser, pick a project, and optionally a default region and zone. Choose the region and zone closest to your users or services to keep latency low, because many commands use them as defaults.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0012","card":"q-gcp-005-installing-and-initializing-the-cloud-sd","sec":"gcp-s006","q":"What is the difference between <code>gcloud auth login</code> and <code>gcloud auth application-default login</code>?","a":"<p><code>gcloud auth login</code> authorizes the gcloud CLI itself. <code>gcloud auth application-default login</code> creates Application Default Credentials that client libraries in your code (for example <code>@google-cloud/storage</code>) find automatically. A common mistake is logging in with the first command and then wondering why a local script is not authenticated.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0013","card":"q-gcp-005-installing-and-initializing-the-cloud-sd","sec":"gcp-s006","q":"After installing the Cloud SDK with the install script, the installer asks you to restart the shell. Why?","a":"<p>The installer edits your shell profile to add <code>gcloud</code> to the PATH and enable completion. A running shell has already read its profile, so it needs a restart (for example <code>exec -l $SHELL</code>) to pick up those changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0014","card":"q-gcp-006-three-cli-tools","sec":"gcp-s007","q":"In the Google Cloud SDK, which command-line tool do you use for Cloud Storage, which for BigQuery, and which for everything else?","a":"<p><code>gsutil</code> for Cloud Storage, <code>bq</code> for BigQuery, and <code>gcloud</code> for the rest. Version note: Google now recommends <code>gcloud storage</code> commands for Cloud Storage, which are faster for large transfers, and <code>gsutil</code> is in maintenance mode, so prefer <code>gcloud storage</code> for new scripts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0015","card":"q-gcp-006-three-cli-tools","sec":"gcp-s007","q":"How would you copy a local file to a Cloud Storage bucket, using both the older and the newer CLI?","a":"<p>Older: <code>gsutil cp file.jpg gs://my-bucket/</code>. Newer (recommended): <code>gcloud storage cp file.jpg gs://my-bucket/</code>. Both take a local path and a <code>gs://</code> URL, and both can copy in the other direction.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0016","card":"q-gcp-007-gcloud-concepts","sec":"gcp-s008","q":"In the gcloud CLI, what are command groups, commands, configurations and properties?","a":"<p>Command groups hold commands or further groups (for example <code>gcloud compute instances</code>). Commands do the work. A configuration is a named set of properties, and the starting one is called <code>default</code>. A property is one setting in <code>section/property</code> form, such as <code>core/project</code> or <code>compute/region</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0017","card":"q-gcp-007-gcloud-concepts","sec":"gcp-s008","q":"Why would you create several gcloud configurations?","a":"<p>To switch between projects, accounts or regions in one command (<code>gcloud config configurations activate &lt;name&gt;</code>) instead of setting and unsetting each property by hand. A typical setup is one configuration each for dev, staging and production, which also lowers the risk of running a command against the wrong project.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0018","card":"q-gcp-007-gcloud-concepts","sec":"gcp-s008","q":"What do the <code>alpha</code> and <code>beta</code> command groups in gcloud mean, and should you use them in production scripts?","a":"<p>They hold commands for features still in development. Alpha is early and can change or be removed without notice; beta is closer to release but can still change. Avoid them in production automation unless you accept breakage. Use <code>gcloud alpha ...</code> or <code>gcloud beta ...</code> to reach them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0019","card":"q-gcp-007-gcloud-concepts","sec":"gcp-s008","q":"How do you check which account gcloud is using and what the current settings are?","a":"<p><code>gcloud auth list</code> shows logged-in accounts and marks the active one with an asterisk. <code>gcloud config list</code> shows the current properties of the active configuration, and <code>gcloud config list --all</code> also shows unset ones. <code>gcloud config configurations list</code> shows all named configurations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0020","card":"q-gcp-007-gcloud-concepts","sec":"gcp-s008","q":"In the gcloud CLI, how do you set and remove a property?","a":"<p>Set with <code>gcloud config set section/property value</code>, for example <code>gcloud config set compute/region us-central1</code>. Remove with <code>gcloud config unset section/property</code>. Use <code>--help</code> on any group or command to see its flags.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0021","card":"q-gcp-008-environments","sec":"gcp-s009","q":"What are the two App Engine environments, and how do they differ?","a":"<p>Standard runs your code in a Google-managed sandbox on supported runtimes. It deploys fast, can scale to zero instances, and is cheaper for spiky traffic, but it limits what you can do (runtimes, filesystem, background processes). Flexible runs your app in a Docker container on Compute Engine VMs. It allows custom runtimes and libraries and is more portable, but it deploys slower and always keeps at least one instance running.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0022","card":"q-gcp-008-environments","sec":"gcp-s009","q":"When would you pick App Engine standard over flexible, and when the reverse?","a":"<p>Choose standard for stateless web apps on a supported runtime, with spiky traffic where scale-to-zero saves money and fast scaling matters. Choose flexible when you need a custom runtime, native libraries, background threads, or a Dockerfile you control. If you need even more control over the container, consider Cloud Run instead.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0023","card":"q-gcp-008-environments","sec":"gcp-s009","q":"What does \"scales to zero\" mean for App Engine standard, and what is the trade-off?","a":"<p>When there is no traffic, no instances run and you pay for no compute. The first request after idle time then starts a new instance, so it sees a cold-start delay. Setting a minimum number of instances avoids cold starts at the price of paying for them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0024","card":"q-gcp-009-minimal-app-files","sec":"gcp-s010","q":"In App Engine, what is the only required property in <code>app.yaml</code>, and what does it do?","a":"<p><code>runtime</code>. It selects the language runtime (for example <code>nodejs20</code> or <code>python312</code>), and App Engine builds and runs your app on it. All other settings such as scaling, handlers and environment variables are optional. Runtime IDs are version-specific and old ones get deprecated, so use a currently supported one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0025","card":"q-gcp-009-minimal-app-files","sec":"gcp-s010","q":"In an App Engine Node.js app, which file defines the command that starts the app, and what does App Engine run by default?","a":"<p><code>package.json</code>, in the <code>scripts.start</code> entry. App Engine runs <code>npm start</code>. If you have no start script it falls back to <code>node server.js</code>, so a missing script is a common cause of a failed deploy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0026","card":"q-gcp-009-minimal-app-files","sec":"gcp-s010","q":"Why must an App Engine Node.js app listen on <code>process.env.PORT</code>?","a":"<p>App Engine tells your instance which port to listen on through the <code>PORT</code> environment variable, and its front end routes requests to that port. A hard-coded port can fail health checks and the deploy. A fallback such as <code>process.env.PORT || 8080</code> keeps local runs working.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>const PORT = process.env.PORT || 8080;\napp.listen(PORT);</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0027","card":"q-gcp-009-minimal-app-files","sec":"gcp-s010","q":"How do you run an Express app locally and then deploy it to App Engine?","a":"<p>Locally: <code>npm start</code>, then open <code>localhost:8080</code>. To deploy, run <code>gcloud app deploy app.yaml</code> from the project folder. gcloud uploads the source, App Engine installs dependencies, builds the version and then serves it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0028","card":"q-gcp-009-minimal-app-files","sec":"gcp-s010","q":"When you run <code>gcloud app deploy</code>, which files should stay out of the upload and how do you control that?","a":"<p>Keep <code>node_modules</code>, <code>.git</code>, local <code>.env</code> files and key files out. Put patterns in <code>.gcloudignore</code>, which gcloud creates with defaults such as <code>.git</code>, <code>.gitignore</code> and <code>node_modules/</code>. App Engine installs dependencies itself from <code>package.json</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0029","card":"q-gcp-010-custom-domain","sec":"gcp-s011","q":"Why do you add a TXT record when mapping a custom domain to App Engine?","a":"<p>Google needs proof that you control the domain before it serves your app there. The TXT record holds a verification token that only the domain owner can place in DNS. Its value does not change, so if DNS has not propagated yet you can retry verification safely.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0030","card":"q-gcp-010-custom-domain","sec":"gcp-s011","q":"After a custom domain is verified and mapped to App Engine, how does HTTPS get enabled?","a":"<p>Google automatically provisions and renews a managed SSL certificate for the mapped domain (it takes a short while after DNS records are in place). You need no extra certificate setup. Check that the DNS records Google lists (A/AAAA for the apex, CNAME for <code>www</code>) are correct, otherwise provisioning stalls.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0031","card":"q-gcp-010-custom-domain","sec":"gcp-s011","q":"Your custom domain was mapped to App Engine but HTTPS still fails after some time. What do you check?","a":"<p>First DNS: confirm the records Google listed are in place and have propagated. Then check that the domain is verified, and that the certificate status in App Engine settings shows as issued. Also look for CAA records that block Google's certificate authority. Provisioning cannot finish until DNS points to App Engine.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0032","card":"q-gcp-011-bring-your-own-ssl-certificate","sec":"gcp-s012","q":"When would you upload your own SSL certificate to App Engine instead of using the managed one?","a":"<p>When you need an organization-issued, extended-validation or specific CA certificate, or a policy requires you to control the certificate. The trade-off is that you must renew and re-upload it before it expires. Managed certificates renew themselves.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0033","card":"q-gcp-011-bring-your-own-ssl-certificate","sec":"gcp-s012","q":"What is the sequence for using your own certificate on an App Engine custom domain?","a":"<p>Verify the domain, disable managed security for it, prepare the key as an unencrypted PEM and the full chain as one PEM file, check that they match, upload both under SSL certificates, and assign the certificate to the domain. Disabling managed security first stops Google from replacing your certificate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0034","card":"q-gcp-011-bring-your-own-ssl-certificate","sec":"gcp-s012","q":"How do you check that an SSL certificate and its private key belong together before uploading them?","a":"<p>Compare the modulus hashes. Both commands must print the same value:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>openssl x509 -noout -modulus -in cert.pem | openssl md5\nopenssl rsa -noout -modulus -in key.pem | openssl md5</code></pre></div>\n<p>A mismatch means the key belongs to a different certificate, and the upload will be rejected.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0035","card":"q-gcp-011-bring-your-own-ssl-certificate","sec":"gcp-s012","q":"Why must the certificate chain be concatenated into one PEM file with each <code>END CERTIFICATE</code> and <code>BEGIN CERTIFICATE</code> on separate lines?","a":"<p>Browsers need the leaf certificate plus the intermediate certificates to build a trust path to a root they know. The file is read as a sequence of PEM blocks, so each block needs its own boundary lines. If they run together on one line, parsing fails or the chain is incomplete, and clients show trust errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0036","card":"q-gcp-011-bring-your-own-ssl-certificate","sec":"gcp-s012","q":"Why does App Engine ask for the private key as an unencrypted PEM, and what is the risk?","a":"<p>The platform has no way to ask for a passphrase at serve time, so it needs the key in readable form. The risk is that the key file is sensitive: keep it out of source control, limit who can read it, and delete local copies after upload. Convert it with <code>openssl rsa -in key.key -out key.pem</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0037","card":"q-gcp-012-hierarchy","sec":"gcp-s013","q":"How is an App Engine application organized, from top to bottom?","a":"<p>A project has one App Engine application, which has one or more services, each service has versions, and each version runs on one or more instances. The service is like a microservice, the version is one deployment, and the instance is the unit of capacity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0038","card":"q-gcp-012-hierarchy","sec":"gcp-s013","q":"Can a Google Cloud project hold more than one App Engine application, and what follows from that?","a":"<p>No, one application per project, and its region is set when you create it and cannot be changed. To get several isolated applications or regions you use several projects. This is also why test and production are usually separate projects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0039","card":"q-gcp-012-hierarchy","sec":"gcp-s013","q":"In App Engine, what is the difference between a service and a version?","a":"<p>A service is a logical component with its own code and config (for example an API, a batch worker or an admin panel). A version is one deployment of a service, so you can keep several versions of a service and shift traffic between them. Versions of one service share the service's name; services scale and deploy independently.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0040","card":"q-gcp-012-hierarchy","sec":"gcp-s013","q":"How are App Engine instances added and removed?","a":"<p>Automatically by an autoscaler based on request rate, latency and CPU, or manually, depending on the scaling type in <code>app.yaml</code> (automatic, basic or manual). Standard can scale to zero. Settings like <code>min_instances</code> and <code>max_instances</code> bound the autoscaler.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0041","card":"q-gcp-013-component-specific-urls","sec":"gcp-s014","q":"In App Engine, how do you send a request to a specific version of a specific service?","a":"<p>Use the URL <code>https://&lt;version&gt;-dot-&lt;service&gt;-dot-&lt;project-id&gt;.&lt;region-id&gt;.r.appspot.com</code>. Targeting one version directly lets QA test a version that gets no production traffic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0042","card":"q-gcp-013-component-specific-urls","sec":"gcp-s014","q":"Why does App Engine use <code>-dot-</code> in service and version URLs instead of normal sub-domains?","a":"<p>The managed SSL certificate for <code>appspot.com</code> domains covers only one level of subdomain. Joining the parts with <code>-dot-</code> keeps everything in one label, so HTTPS stays valid. With real dots you would see certificate warnings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0043","card":"q-gcp-013-component-specific-urls","sec":"gcp-s014","q":"What URL reaches the default service and which version does it serve?","a":"<p><code>https://&lt;project-id&gt;.&lt;region-id&gt;.r.appspot.com</code> reaches the default service. It serves the version that currently receives traffic (the split or migrated one), not necessarily the latest deployed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0044","card":"q-gcp-014-multi-environment-best-practice","sec":"gcp-s015","q":"Why use separate Google Cloud projects for test and production instead of separate App Engine services in one project?","a":"<p>Services in one project share the same data stores (Cloud Storage, Firestore, Cloud SQL access, IAM). A bug or test run could change production data. Separate projects give isolated data, IAM, quotas and billing. The cost is more projects to manage, which CI/CD or infrastructure as code can handle.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0045","card":"q-gcp-014-multi-environment-best-practice","sec":"gcp-s015","q":"What is a good App Engine version naming convention, and why?","a":"<p>Use <code>&lt;timestamp&gt;-&lt;git-commit-hash&gt;</code>. The timestamp gives deployment order and the hash identifies the exact commit, so you can roll back to a known build and trace any version to its code. Version IDs must be lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, so format the timestamp that way.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0046","card":"q-gcp-014-multi-environment-best-practice","sec":"gcp-s015","q":"How can QA test several feature branches in parallel on App Engine?","a":"<p>Deploy each branch as its own version in the test project (for example <code>feature-x</code>) with <code>gcloud app deploy --version feature-x --no-promote</code>, so none takes live traffic. QA opens each version through its own <code>-dot-</code> URL. Delete the versions when the branch is merged.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0047","card":"q-gcp-014-multi-environment-best-practice","sec":"gcp-s015","q":"What does <code>--no-promote</code> do in <code>gcloud app deploy</code>?","a":"<p>It deploys a new version without sending it traffic. The current serving version stays live, and you test the new one by its version URL. Then you migrate or split traffic when ready.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0048","card":"q-gcp-015-traffic-splitting-and-rollback","sec":"gcp-s016","q":"How do the three App Engine traffic-splitting methods (IP address, cookie, random) differ?","a":"<p>IP address routes by the client IP, so a client mostly keeps its version, but users behind one NAT or proxy share a bucket, and IPs change. Cookie routes by a cookie (App Engine uses <code>GOOGAPPUID</code>), which gives the most reliable stickiness for browsers. Random picks per request by percentage, so one user may see different versions on consecutive requests.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0049","card":"q-gcp-015-traffic-splitting-and-rollback","sec":"gcp-s016","q":"You are A/B testing a UI change and users must keep seeing the same version during a session. Which split method do you choose and why?","a":"<p>Cookie splitting, because a browser sends the cookie on every request and so stays on one version. IP splitting is a weaker fallback, and random would flip users between versions. For API clients that do not keep cookies, use IP.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0050","card":"q-gcp-015-traffic-splitting-and-rollback","sec":"gcp-s016","q":"How do you roll back a bad release on App Engine?","a":"<p>Migrate 100% of the traffic back to the earlier version, which is still deployed. Rollback is the same operation as a release, just pointed at the old version, so it is quick and needs no rebuild. This only works while old versions are kept. For example: <code>gcloud app services set-traffic default --splits v1=1</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0051","card":"q-gcp-015-traffic-splitting-and-rollback","sec":"gcp-s016","q":"How would you send 10% of traffic to a new App Engine version from the command line?","a":"<p><code>gcloud app services set-traffic default --splits v1=0.9,v2=0.1 --split-by cookie</code>. The fractions must sum to 1, and <code>--split-by</code> picks the method (<code>ip</code>, <code>cookie</code> or <code>random</code>). You can then raise the share step by step if the metrics stay healthy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0052","card":"q-gcp-015-traffic-splitting-and-rollback","sec":"gcp-s016","q":"What is the difference between splitting traffic and migrating traffic in App Engine?","a":"<p>Splitting divides traffic across several versions by percentage and keeps it that way. Migrating moves all traffic to one version. In standard, migration can be gradual (<code>--migrate</code>), which warms up the new version as traffic shifts, instead of switching all at once.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0053","card":"q-gcp-016-cloud-sql","sec":"gcp-s017","q":"What does Cloud SQL manage for you, and what do you still own?","a":"<p>Cloud SQL is a managed relational database service for MySQL, PostgreSQL and SQL Server. Google runs patching, backups, replication, failover and storage growth. You still own the schema, queries and indexes, access control and users, connection management, capacity choices and cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0054","card":"q-gcp-016-cloud-sql","sec":"gcp-s017","q":"Which Cloud SQL instance settings cannot be changed after creation, and how should that affect planning?","a":"<p>The region is permanent; the zone can be changed. Put the database in the same region as the services that use it, to keep latency low and avoid cross-region egress cost. Moving regions later means creating a new instance and migrating the data (for example from a backup or a replica).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0055","card":"q-gcp-016-cloud-sql","sec":"gcp-s017","q":"What are the options for Cloud SQL connectivity, private IP versus public IP?","a":"<p>Private IP is reachable only from your VPC (and connected networks) and gives lower exposure and latency. Public IP is reachable from the internet, so you restrict it with authorized networks or, better, connect through the Cloud SQL Auth Proxy, which encrypts and authenticates with IAM. Prefer private IP for production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0056","card":"q-gcp-016-cloud-sql","sec":"gcp-s017","q":"What does high availability mean in Cloud SQL, and is it the same as a read replica?","a":"<p>HA keeps a standby in another zone of the same region and fails over automatically when the primary zone has trouble, with storage kept in sync. It is for availability. A read replica is a separate copy that serves read traffic to scale reads and is not an automatic failover target in the same way. Use HA for production and replicas for read scaling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0057","card":"q-gcp-016-cloud-sql","sec":"gcp-s017","q":"What is the difference between automatic backups and point-in-time recovery in Cloud SQL?","a":"<p>Automatic backups are scheduled snapshots, so you can restore only to those moments. Point-in-time recovery uses the transaction logs on top of a backup to restore to any chosen moment, for example just before an accidental delete. It needs backups to be on and binary logging (MySQL) or WAL archiving (PostgreSQL), which Cloud SQL manages for you.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0058","card":"q-gcp-016-cloud-sql","sec":"gcp-s017","q":"How do you pick the machine type and storage type for a Cloud SQL instance?","a":"<p>Start from the workload: the vCPU and RAM size determine connections, caching and network throughput. SSD gives low-latency IO and suits most uses; HDD is cheaper for large, rarely read data. Disk throughput grows with disk size. Turn on automatic storage increases so you do not run out of space.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0059","card":"q-gcp-017-service-accounts-iam","sec":"gcp-s018","q":"What is a service account in Google Cloud and why is it better than a user account for applications?","a":"<p>A service account is an identity for software, not a person. It has no password, you grant it only the roles it needs, and you can rotate or disable it without affecting a human. Applications on Google Cloud use an attached service account to call other services with no keys in code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0060","card":"q-gcp-017-service-accounts-iam","sec":"gcp-s018","q":"What is the default App Engine service account, and how do you let your app use Cloud SQL?","a":"<p>The default account is <code>&lt;project-id&gt;@appspot.gserviceaccount.com</code>. Grant it the <code>Cloud SQL Client</code> role in IAM, which is enough to connect. Extra roles like Editor or Admin also work but give far more access than needed. Prefer a dedicated service account with the minimum roles for production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0061","card":"q-gcp-017-service-accounts-iam","sec":"gcp-s018","q":"Why does a service account key file need careful handling, and what is the safer alternative?","a":"<p>A JSON key is a long-lived credential: anyone holding the file acts as the account, and it is shown only once at creation. Do not commit it or ship it in images. The safer alternative is to attach the service account to the runtime (App Engine, Cloud Run, GCE) so credentials come from the metadata server, or use workload identity federation outside Google Cloud.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0062","card":"q-gcp-017-service-accounts-iam","sec":"gcp-s018","q":"How do disabling and deleting a service account differ?","a":"<p>Disabling is temporary and reversible: its credentials stop working, and you can re-enable it. Deleting is permanent (recovery is only possible for a short period and not guaranteed). Disable first when you suspect misuse or are unsure what depends on it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0063","card":"q-gcp-017-service-accounts-iam","sec":"gcp-s018","q":"What is the principle of least privilege when assigning IAM roles to a service account?","a":"<p>Grant only the roles the workload needs, at the narrowest scope (resource or project rather than organization). Predefined roles such as <code>Cloud SQL Client</code> are preferred over basic roles like Editor or Owner. That limits damage if the account is misused.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0064","card":"q-gcp-018-connecting-from-app-engine-unix-socket","sec":"gcp-s019","q":"How does an App Engine app connect to Cloud SQL without an IP address or a proxy process?","a":"<p>Through a Unix domain socket at <code>/cloudsql/&lt;INSTANCE_CONNECTION_NAME&gt;</code>, which App Engine provides in the runtime. The app authenticates with its service account, which needs the Cloud SQL Client role, and the connection is encrypted by the platform. The Cloud SQL Admin API must be enabled.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0065","card":"q-gcp-018-connecting-from-app-engine-unix-socket","sec":"gcp-s019","q":"What is the INSTANCE_CONNECTION_NAME of a Cloud SQL instance and how is it formatted?","a":"<p>It uniquely identifies the instance as <code>project-id:region:instance-name</code>. It is used for the socket path and by the Cloud SQL Proxy. A wrong value is a typical cause of \"connection refused\" or \"no such file\" errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0066","card":"q-gcp-018-connecting-from-app-engine-unix-socket","sec":"gcp-s019","q":"In App Engine <code>app.yaml</code>, what are <code>env_variables</code> for, and why do they not apply when you run the app locally?","a":"<p>They define environment variables that App Engine injects into the deployed instances, for example the database name and connection name. Locally, <code>app.yaml</code> is not read by <code>npm start</code>, so you use a <code>.env</code> file or your shell instead. Do not keep real passwords in <code>app.yaml</code> committed to Git; use Secret Manager.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0067","card":"q-gcp-018-connecting-from-app-engine-unix-socket","sec":"gcp-s019","q":"In a Node.js <code>mysql</code> pool config, what is <code>socketPath</code> for, and what changes when you use <code>host</code> and <code>port</code> instead?","a":"<p><code>socketPath</code> makes the driver connect over a Unix socket file, such as <code>/cloudsql/project:region:instance</code>, which is how a deployed App Engine app reaches Cloud SQL. <code>host</code> and <code>port</code> make it use TCP, for example to a local Cloud SQL Proxy on <code>localhost:3306</code>. You use one or the other, not both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0068","card":"q-gcp-018-connecting-from-app-engine-unix-socket","sec":"gcp-s019","q":"How can one Node.js codebase connect to Cloud SQL by Unix socket when deployed on App Engine, and by TCP through the Cloud SQL Proxy when run locally?","a":"<p>Check a variable that exists only locally, such as <code>DB_HOST</code>. If it is set, build a TCP pool to <code>localhost:3306</code> through the proxy. Otherwise build a Unix socket pool for the deployed environment. One codebase works in both places, and only the environment differs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0069","card":"q-gcp-018-connecting-from-app-engine-unix-socket","sec":"gcp-s019","q":"What is a pitfall of creating a new MySQL connection pool inside a request handler?","a":"<p>Each request builds a new pool with new connections, which are never closed. Under load this exhausts connection limits on the instance. Create the pool once at module level and reuse it, with a small <code>connectionLimit</code> because each App Engine instance holds its own pool.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>app.get(&#x27;/&#x27;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const pool = await createPool({});\n  pool.query(&#x27;SELECT 1 + 1 AS solution&#x27;, function (error, results, fields) {\n    ...\n  });\n});</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"gcp-f0070","card":"q-gcp-019-cloud-sql-proxy-local-development","sec":"gcp-s020","q":"Why do you need the Cloud SQL Proxy for local development with a Cloud SQL public IP instance?","a":"<p>Locally the App Engine service account and the <code>/cloudsql</code> socket do not exist. The proxy authenticates with your gcloud credentials, opens an encrypted tunnel to the instance and exposes it on <code>localhost:3306</code>. You need no authorized-network IP list, and traffic is encrypted with TLS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0071","card":"q-gcp-019-cloud-sql-proxy-local-development","sec":"gcp-s020","q":"What do you need in place before the Cloud SQL Proxy can connect?","a":"<p>A Cloud SQL instance, an active account in gcloud (check <code>gcloud auth list</code>) with enough rights (at least the Cloud SQL Client role), the Cloud SQL Admin API enabled in the project, and a MySQL client or app to connect to the local port. Missing API enablement is a very common failure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0072","card":"q-gcp-019-cloud-sql-proxy-local-development","sec":"gcp-s020","q":"How do you start the Cloud SQL Proxy for MySQL on local port 3306?","a":"<p>Version note: with the v2 binary, <code>./cloud-sql-proxy &lt;project-id&gt;:&lt;region&gt;:&lt;instance-name&gt; --port 3306</code>. The older v1 binary used <code>./cloud_sql_proxy -instances=&lt;connection-name&gt;=tcp:3306</code>. The proxy then listens on <code>localhost:3306</code> and your app uses <code>localhost</code> as the DB host.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0073","card":"q-gcp-019-cloud-sql-proxy-local-development","sec":"gcp-s020","q":"Why use different databases for production and development when working through the Cloud SQL Proxy?","a":"<p>The proxy gives your laptop a direct line to the real instance, so a bad query or migration run locally would hit live data. Use a separate instance or at least a separate database and user with limited rights for development.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0074","card":"q-gcp-020-cloud-sql-proxy-local-development","sec":"gcp-s020","q":"In a Node.js app using <code>dotenv</code>, why is <code>require('dotenv').config()</code> called first, and why must the <code>.env</code> file stay out of deployments?","a":"<p><code>dotenv</code> loads variables from <code>.env</code> into <code>process.env</code>, so it must run before any code that reads them. The <code>.env</code> holds local-only values like <code>DB_HOST=localhost:3306</code>. If deployed, the app would take the TCP branch and fail on App Engine, and secrets would leak into the build. Add it to <code>.gcloudignore</code> and <code>.gitignore</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0075","card":"q-gcp-020-cloud-sql-proxy-local-development","sec":"gcp-s020","q":"When a local Node.js app sets <code>DB_HOST=\"localhost:3306\"</code> but the code needs a host and a port, how can it handle that?","a":"<p>Split the string on the colon: <code>const [host, port] = process.env.DB_HOST.split(':')</code>, then pass <code>host</code> and <code>port</code> to <code>mysql.createPool</code>. This keeps one variable for the endpoint. A separate <code>DB_PORT</code> variable is a more explicit alternative.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0076","card":"q-gcp-020-cloud-sql-proxy-local-development","sec":"gcp-s020","q":"Who authenticates and encrypts the connection to Cloud SQL when an app uses the <code>/cloudsql</code> Unix socket on App Engine, compared with TCP to <code>localhost:3306</code> through the Cloud SQL Proxy?","a":"<p>On App Engine the platform's built-in connector authenticates as the app's service account, which needs the Cloud SQL Client role. Locally the proxy process authenticates with your gcloud credentials, which need the same role. In both cases the proxy or connector encrypts the traffic to the instance, so you do not manage SSL certificates or authorized IP lists. Only the connection settings in the app differ.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0077","card":"q-gcp-021-6-cloud-storage-gcs","sec":"gcp-s021","q":"What are buckets and objects in Cloud Storage, and why must bucket names be globally unique?","a":"<p>A bucket is a container for data and an object is a file plus its metadata. The bucket name is part of every object's URL (<code>gs://bucket/object</code> or <code>storage.googleapis.com/bucket/object</code>), so it must be unique across all of Google Cloud. Pick a name you will not need to change, because a bucket cannot be renamed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0078","card":"q-gcp-021-6-cloud-storage-gcs","sec":"gcp-s021","q":"Does Cloud Storage have real folders?","a":"<p>No. The namespace is flat. A name like <code>images/2024/a.jpg</code> is just an object name with slashes, and the console and tools show a folder view by treating the slash as a delimiter. The consequence is that renaming a \"folder\" means copying and deleting every object under that prefix.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0079","card":"q-gcp-021-6-cloud-storage-gcs","sec":"gcp-s021","q":"When would you choose Cloud Storage over a database or a file system for data?","a":"<p>Use it for unstructured or large objects (images, videos, backups, logs, static website files) that you read as whole objects. It scales without limit, is cheap and durable, and serves over HTTP. Do not use it for relational queries or frequent small in-place edits; objects are immutable and replaced as a whole.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0080","card":"q-gcp-022-location-types","sec":"gcp-s022","q":"What are the three Cloud Storage location types, and how do you choose among them?","a":"<p>Region is a single region: cheapest and lowest latency to compute in that region. Dual-region is two specific regions in one geography: higher availability and geo-redundancy at higher cost. Multi-region is a large geography such as US or EU: highest availability and good for content served to users spread across it, at the highest storage price. Choose by availability need, user location and budget.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0081","card":"q-gcp-022-location-types","sec":"gcp-s022","q":"Why does the Cloud Storage location of a bucket matter for cost and performance, and can you change it?","a":"<p>Data close to the compute that reads it has lower latency and avoids cross-region network egress charges. The location is set when the bucket is created and cannot be changed. To move data you create a new bucket and copy the objects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0082","card":"q-gcp-023-storage-classes","sec":"gcp-s023","q":"What are the Cloud Storage storage classes and how do their costs trade off?","a":"<p>Standard, Nearline, Coldline and Archive. Moving from Standard to Archive, the storage price per GB falls, while retrieval fees rise and minimum storage durations apply (30 days Nearline, 90 Coldline, 365 Archive). Standard has no minimum duration and no retrieval fee. Use Standard for hot data, Nearline for monthly access, Coldline for quarterly and Archive for yearly or less.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0083","card":"q-gcp-023-storage-classes","sec":"gcp-s023","q":"Is Archive storage in Cloud Storage slow to read, like tape?","a":"<p>No. All classes return data in milliseconds with the same API and latency. The difference is price: Archive has the lowest storage cost but the highest retrieval cost and a 365-day minimum charge. That is why it fits backups and compliance data that you rarely read.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0084","card":"q-gcp-023-storage-classes","sec":"gcp-s023","q":"What are Class A and Class B operations in Cloud Storage and why do they matter?","a":"<p>Class A operations modify or list: creating buckets and objects, uploads, changing permissions, listing. Class B operations read: downloads and metadata reads. Class A is billed higher than Class B. Many small writes can cost more than expected, so batch or compose objects when possible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0085","card":"q-gcp-023-storage-classes","sec":"gcp-s023","q":"Which Cloud Storage option stops anyone from deleting an object before a set time, and what is it for?","a":"<p>A retention policy on the bucket. Objects cannot be deleted or replaced until they reach the retention age. It is used for compliance such as financial records. Locking the policy makes it permanent, so lock only when sure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0086","card":"q-gcp-023-storage-classes","sec":"gcp-s023","q":"What encryption options does Cloud Storage offer?","a":"<p>All data is encrypted at rest by default with Google-managed keys. You can use customer-managed encryption keys held in Cloud KMS for control over rotation and revocation, or customer-supplied keys that you pass on each request. More key control means more operational duty: losing the key means losing the data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0087","card":"q-gcp-024-upload-download-methods","sec":"gcp-s024","q":"What are the ways to upload and download objects in Cloud Storage?","a":"<p>The Cloud Console, the CLI (<code>gsutil cp</code> or <code>gcloud storage cp</code>), client libraries in many languages, and the JSON and XML REST APIs. Applications normally use client libraries, scripts use the CLI, and the console suits one-off tasks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0088","card":"q-gcp-024-upload-download-methods","sec":"gcp-s024","q":"How do you let a browser upload a large file straight to Cloud Storage without passing through your server?","a":"<p>Generate a signed upload URL (action <code>write</code>) on the server and give it to the client, which sends a PUT to it. Your server only signs and never carries the data, so you save bandwidth and server time. For huge files use a resumable upload session.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0089","card":"q-gcp-025-access-control-uniform-vs-fine-grained","sec":"gcp-s025","q":"What is the difference between uniform and fine-grained access control on a Cloud Storage bucket?","a":"<p>Uniform bucket-level access uses IAM only, and every object inherits the bucket permissions. Fine-grained also allows per-object ACLs, so one object can be public while another is private. Uniform is recommended: it is simpler to audit and enables features such as IAM Conditions. Fine-grained is easy to get wrong.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0090","card":"q-gcp-025-access-control-uniform-vs-fine-grained","sec":"gcp-s025","q":"How do you make every object in a bucket publicly readable under uniform access, and how do you undo it?","a":"<p>Grant <code>allUsers</code> the <code>Storage Object Viewer</code> role on the bucket and confirm the public access prompt. To make objects private again, remove that binding. Public access prevention can be turned on to block this kind of grant entirely.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0091","card":"q-gcp-025-access-control-uniform-vs-fine-grained","sec":"gcp-s025","q":"What is the risk when you switch a bucket from uniform back to fine-grained?","a":"<p>Existing object ACLs set earlier become active again. They can grant broader access than the bucket's IAM policy, so objects you thought were private may be exposed. Audit ACLs before switching back. Also, Google only allows reverting for a limited period (90 days) after enabling uniform access.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0092","card":"q-gcp-025-access-control-uniform-vs-fine-grained","sec":"gcp-s025","q":"Why is a bucket with public objects and fine-grained ACLs a security concern?","a":"<p>Access is spread across thousands of per-object settings that no single view shows, so it is hard to audit and easy to leave something exposed. With uniform access you check one IAM policy for the whole bucket.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0093","card":"q-gcp-026-signed-urls","sec":"gcp-s026","q":"What is a Cloud Storage signed URL and when do you use one?","a":"<p>A signed URL grants time-limited access to one private object to anyone who holds the link, without a Google account. Use it to share downloads (invoices, reports) or allow uploads from clients while the bucket stays private. The link carries the permission, so treat it like a password until it expires.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0094","card":"q-gcp-026-signed-urls","sec":"gcp-s026","q":"How does the signed URL flow work between a user, your application and Cloud Storage?","a":"<p>The user asks your app for a file. The app, after its own authorization check, signs a URL with a service account and returns it. The user's client then fetches the object directly from Cloud Storage, so the file bytes never pass through your server.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0095","card":"q-gcp-026-signed-urls","sec":"gcp-s026","q":"What is the maximum validity of a V4 signed URL and what does the URL contain?","a":"<p>Up to 7 days. The URL holds the object path, the signing algorithm, the credential (service account), the start date, the expiry in seconds from the start, the signed headers and the signature. Changing any signed part invalidates the signature.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0096","card":"q-gcp-026-signed-urls","sec":"gcp-s026","q":"What permissions does the signing service account need for a signed read URL?","a":"<p>The account needs permission to read the object, such as the <code>Storage Object Viewer</code> role (<code>storage.objects.get</code>), because the URL acts with the account's rights. Signing needs its private key, or the IAM signBlob capability in Google Cloud. Locally you pass the key file; on GCP the runtime's default service account is used.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0097","card":"q-gcp-026-signed-urls","sec":"gcp-s026","q":"In Node.js with <code>@google-cloud/storage</code>, how do you create a signed URL for reading an object?","a":"<p><code>getSignedUrl</code> on a file object takes options: <code>version: 'v4'</code>, <code>action</code> (<code>read</code> or <code>write</code>) and <code>expires</code> as a time in milliseconds. This code makes the URL expire after 15 minutes. <code>keyFilename</code> supplies the key locally; on App Engine you omit it and the default service account is used. The call returns an array whose first element is the URL.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>const {Storage} = require(&#x27;@google-cloud/storage&#x27;);\nconst storage = new Storage({ keyFilename: &#x27;&lt;service-account-key&gt;.json&#x27; });\nconst options = {\n  version: &#x27;v4&#x27;,\n  action: &#x27;read&#x27;,\n  expires: Date.now() + 15 * 60 * 1000,\n};\nconst [url] = await storage.bucket(bucketName).file(filename).getSignedUrl(options);</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"gcp-f0098","card":"q-gcp-026-signed-urls","sec":"gcp-s026","q":"What changes in the <code>getSignedUrl</code> options to allow an upload instead of a download?","a":"<p>Set <code>action: 'write'</code>. The client then sends an HTTP PUT with the file to the signed URL. If you also sign a <code>contentType</code>, the client must send exactly that header, otherwise the signature check fails.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0099","card":"q-gcp-026-signed-urls","sec":"gcp-s026","q":"How would you create a signed URL from the command line for a quick test?","a":"<p><code>gsutil signurl -d 10m &lt;service-account-key&gt;.json gs://&lt;bucket-name&gt;/&lt;file-name&gt;</code>. The <code>-d</code> flag sets how long the URL stays valid and the key file proves who signs it. Anyone who gets that URL can read the object until it expires.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0100","card":"q-gcp-027-object-lifecycle-management","sec":"gcp-s027","q":"How do Cloud Storage lifecycle rules work: what are the actions and conditions?","a":"<p>A rule pairs an action with conditions. Actions are set storage class (for example Standard to Archive) and delete. Conditions include age in days, creation date, current storage class, and number of newer versions or live state when versioning is on. Rules apply to all objects in the bucket that match, and age counts from creation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0101","card":"q-gcp-027-object-lifecycle-management","sec":"gcp-s027","q":"What happens in Cloud Storage when an object matches several lifecycle rules at once?","a":"<p>The action that is cheapest to keep wins. Delete beats a class change, and among class changes the colder (cheaper) class wins. This avoids two rules fighting over one object.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0102","card":"q-gcp-027-object-lifecycle-management","sec":"gcp-s027","q":"How would you configure a bucket for a file-download service so files go cold after 30 days and disappear after a year?","a":"<p>Rule 1: set storage class to Archive when age is 30 days and the current class is Standard. Rule 2: delete when age is 365 days. Check that any minimum storage duration charges for the class you move to are acceptable, and that links to deleted files are expected to stop working.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0103","card":"q-gcp-027-object-lifecycle-management","sec":"gcp-s027","q":"Why might a lifecycle rule that moves objects to Nearline or Coldline increase your bill?","a":"<p>Colder classes have minimum storage durations (30 or 90 days) and retrieval fees. If objects are deleted or read soon after the move, you are billed for the minimum duration and for retrieval. Use these rules only for data whose access pattern really is rare.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0104","card":"q-gcp-027-object-lifecycle-management","sec":"gcp-s027","q":"Which lifecycle conditions are relevant when object versioning is on?","a":"<p>Number of newer versions and live state. They let you delete old noncurrent versions (for example keep only 3) while leaving the live object. Without such rules, versioned buckets keep growing and you pay for every version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0105","card":"q-gcp-028-concepts","sec":"gcp-s028","q":"What is Cloud Tasks, and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>It is a managed service for running asynchronous work through queues. Your code adds tasks (an HTTP request with a payload) and the service dispatches them to a handler at a controlled rate, with retries. It separates producing work from doing it, so a web request returns fast and heavy or slow work happens in the background without overwhelming your backend.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0106","card":"q-gcp-028-concepts","sec":"gcp-s028","q":"What are the two Cloud Tasks target types, and when do you use each?","a":"<p>App Engine tasks call a handler by relative URI inside an App Engine app. HTTP target tasks call any public or authenticated HTTP endpoint, such as Cloud Run, Cloud Functions or your own server. Use HTTP targets for anything outside App Engine.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0107","card":"q-gcp-028-concepts","sec":"gcp-s028","q":"How do Cloud Tasks rate limit settings differ: max dispatches per second, max concurrent dispatches and max burst size?","a":"<p>Max dispatches per second limits how many tasks start each second. Max concurrent dispatches caps how many run at the same time, which protects the handler and downstream systems. Max burst size is the token bucket depth, so a queue that was idle can send a short burst before settling at the steady rate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0108","card":"q-gcp-028-concepts","sec":"gcp-s028","q":"How does Cloud Tasks retry a failed task, and how do you configure it?","a":"<p>A task fails when the handler returns a non-2xx status or times out. Cloud Tasks then retries with exponential backoff: wait min backoff, double up to a set number of times, cap at max backoff, until max attempts is reached. Set these per queue. Lower max attempts for work that should not repeat many times.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0109","card":"q-gcp-028-concepts","sec":"gcp-s028","q":"Cloud Tasks may deliver a task more than once. How should the handler be written?","a":"<p>Make it idempotent. Cloud Tasks guarantees at-least-once delivery, so retries or rare duplicates can run the handler twice. Use a unique key (a task name or an ID in the payload) and check whether the work was already done, for example before sending an email or charging a card.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0110","card":"q-gcp-028-concepts","sec":"gcp-s028","q":"In the Cloud Tasks console, how do pausing a queue, running a task, deleting a task and purging a queue differ?","a":"<p>Pause stops dispatch and keeps the tasks, and Resume restarts it. Run now forces one task to run immediately. Delete removes a single task. Purge removes all tasks in the queue. Pause is the safe choice when a downstream system is failing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0111","card":"q-gcp-028-concepts","sec":"gcp-s028","q":"How do you create a Cloud Tasks queue and inspect it with gcloud?","a":"<p><code>gcloud tasks queues create &lt;name&gt;</code> creates it and <code>gcloud tasks queues describe &lt;name&gt;</code> shows its config. The Cloud Tasks API must be enabled in the project. A queue's location is fixed when it is created.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0112","card":"q-gcp-028-concepts","sec":"gcp-s028","q":"When would you use Cloud Tasks and when Pub/Sub?","a":"<p>Cloud Tasks suits explicit targeting of one handler, with control of rate, scheduling, retries and deduplication by task name. Pub/Sub suits event fan-out to many subscribers who decide what to do. Use Cloud Tasks for job dispatch to a known service, and Pub/Sub for decoupled events.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0113","card":"q-gcp-029-creating-a-task-from-app-engine-exercise","sec":"gcp-s029","q":"In the Cloud Tasks Node.js client, what does the <code>appEngineHttpRequest</code> field of a task describe?","a":"<p>It describes the HTTP request Cloud Tasks will make to your App Engine app: <code>httpMethod</code> (such as POST), <code>relativeUri</code> (the handler path) and an optional <code>body</code>. Cloud Tasks sends it to the default service unless you set <code>appEngineRouting</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0114","card":"q-gcp-029-creating-a-task-from-app-engine-exercise","sec":"gcp-s029","q":"Why is the task payload base64-encoded in <code>body</code> in this Cloud Tasks code?","a":"<p>The <code>body</code> field is bytes, which the JSON form of the API carries as base64 text. The code turns the object into a JSON string, then into bytes, then into base64. The handler receives the original JSON again as the request body, so set a JSON content type if it should parse automatically.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>task.appEngineHttpRequest.body =\n  Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(payload)).toString(&#x27;base64&#x27;);</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"gcp-f0115","card":"q-gcp-029-creating-a-task-from-app-engine-exercise","sec":"gcp-s029","q":"How do you delay a Cloud Tasks task so it runs later?","a":"<p>Set <code>scheduleTime</code> on the task, a timestamp with seconds since the epoch. Without it the task is eligible immediately. For a delay of N seconds use the current time in seconds plus N.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0116","card":"q-gcp-029-creating-a-task-from-app-engine-exercise","sec":"gcp-s029","q":"In the Cloud Tasks Node.js client, what does <code>client.queuePath(project, location, queue)</code> return and why is it needed?","a":"<p>It builds the full resource name <code>projects/&lt;project&gt;/locations/&lt;location&gt;/queues/&lt;queue&gt;</code>. The API requires that string as the <code>parent</code> of the <code>createTask</code> request. A wrong location string gives a \"queue not found\" error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0117","card":"q-gcp-029-creating-a-task-from-app-engine-exercise","sec":"gcp-s029","q":"In Express, why parse the task request body with <code>body-parser</code> and keep the raw body, as in this setup?","a":"<p>The <code>verify</code> callback runs while the body is read and lets you keep the raw text in <code>req.rawBody</code>, while <code>bodyParser.json</code> still fills <code>req.body</code>. This helps when a payload does not parse as expected or when you need the exact bytes, for example to check a signature. Tasks may be sent with other content types, so extra parsers cover them.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>const rawBodySaver = (req, res, buf, encoding) =&gt; {\n  if (buf &amp;&amp; buf.length) {\n    req.rawBody = buf.toString(encoding || &#x27;utf8&#x27;);\n  }\n};\napp.use(bodyParser.json({ verify: rawBodySaver }));</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"gcp-f0118","card":"q-gcp-029-creating-a-task-from-app-engine-exercise","sec":"gcp-s029","q":"What does <code>app.enable('trust proxy')</code> do in an Express app on App Engine?","a":"<p>App Engine puts a front-end proxy between clients and your instance. With <code>trust proxy</code>, Express reads <code>X-Forwarded-*</code> headers, so <code>req.ip</code>, <code>req.protocol</code> and <code>req.secure</code> reflect the real client and HTTPS, not the proxy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0119","card":"q-gcp-029-creating-a-task-from-app-engine-exercise","sec":"gcp-s029","q":"How does an App Engine handler know a request came from Cloud Tasks and not from the internet?","a":"<p>Cloud Tasks requests to App Engine carry headers such as <code>X-AppEngine-QueueName</code> and <code>X-AppEngine-TaskName</code>. App Engine strips these headers from external requests, so a handler can trust them. You can also restrict the route with <code>login: admin</code> in <code>app.yaml</code> handlers (a standard-environment feature).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0120","card":"q-gcp-030-emailing-subscribers-fan-out-pattern-exe","sec":"gcp-s030","q":"Why does looping over all subscribers and sending an email in one request not scale on App Engine?","a":"<p>The request has a deadline, and one instance does all the work serially. With tens of thousands of recipients the request times out, loses progress and cannot be resumed cleanly. Sending work should be split into tasks that run in parallel and retry on their own.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0121","card":"q-gcp-030-emailing-subscribers-fan-out-pattern-exe","sec":"gcp-s030","q":"Compare sending emails directly, one Cloud Task per email, and the fan-out pattern. What are the trade-offs?","a":"<p>Direct: simple, but it times out and does not scale. Task per email: each email is retried alone and handled in parallel, but one request still has to create all the tasks, so that loop can time out. Fan-out: the first request creates a small number of generator tasks, and each one creates the send tasks for its batch. Creation is parallel too, so it scales best, at the cost of more moving parts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0122","card":"q-gcp-030-emailing-subscribers-fan-out-pattern-exe","sec":"gcp-s030","q":"What is the fan-out pattern with Cloud Tasks, and how do you size it?","a":"<p>Tasks that create tasks. A coordinator creates one generator task per batch. Each generator task gets a start offset and a batch size, reads its slice of the data and creates the individual work tasks. For 100,000 recipients at batch size 100, that is 1,000 generator tasks. Pick a batch size that finishes within the handler deadline.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0123","card":"q-gcp-030-emailing-subscribers-fan-out-pattern-exe","sec":"gcp-s030","q":"Why do you create several queues for a fan-out job (a generator queue and a sender queue)?","a":"<p>Each queue has its own rate limits and retry settings. The generators are few and heavy on the database; the senders are many and call an email provider. Separate queues let you cap concurrency for each stage separately, and pause or purge one without affecting the other.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0124","card":"q-gcp-030-emailing-subscribers-fan-out-pattern-exe","sec":"gcp-s030","q":"How do you protect a downstream email provider or database from being overwhelmed by a fan-out of thousands of tasks?","a":"<p>Set the queue's max dispatches per second and max concurrent dispatches. Cloud Tasks then feeds the handlers at that pace however many tasks are queued. Also make handlers idempotent so retries do not send duplicates.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0125","card":"q-gcp-031-emailing-subscribers-fan-out-pattern-exe","sec":"gcp-s030","q":"Why is <code>LIMIT ?, ?</code> with no <code>ORDER BY</code> risky for fetching batches of rows in a fan-out generator task?","a":"<p>Without <code>ORDER BY</code>, MySQL gives no guarantee of row order, so different queries can return overlapping or missing rows across batches. Add <code>ORDER BY id</code>. Large offsets are also slow because the database still scans the skipped rows; keyset paging (<code>WHERE id &gt; ? ORDER BY id LIMIT ?</code>) scales better.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>const sql = &quot;SELECT email from emails LIMIT ?, ?&quot;;\nconst [results] = await pool.query(sql, [startCount, limit]);</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"gcp-f0126","card":"q-gcp-031-emailing-subscribers-fan-out-pattern-exe","sec":"gcp-s030","q":"Why use placeholders (<code>?</code>) with <code>pool.query(sql, [values])</code> in the <code>mysql</code> and <code>mysql2</code> libraries?","a":"<p>The driver escapes values separately from the SQL text, which prevents SQL injection and handles types correctly. Never build the query by joining strings with user input.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0127","card":"q-gcp-031-emailing-subscribers-fan-out-pattern-exe","sec":"gcp-s030","q":"What is the difference between the <code>mysql</code> and <code>mysql2</code> packages, and what does <code>pool.promise()</code> do?","a":"<p><code>mysql2</code> is a faster, actively maintained driver with the same callback API plus a promise API. <code>pool.promise()</code> wraps the pool so <code>await pool.query(...)</code> returns <code>[rows, fields]</code> without callbacks. The older <code>mysql</code> package is callback-only.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0128","card":"q-gcp-031-emailing-subscribers-fan-out-pattern-exe","sec":"gcp-s030","q":"Why should a helper that sends emails be a separate module from the HTTP route code in an Express app?","a":"<p>Then the sending logic can be swapped (a real email provider instead of a stub), tested alone and reused from several routes or task handlers. The routes then only deal with HTTP concerns.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0129","card":"q-gcp-032-emailing-subscribers-fan-out-pattern-exe","sec":"gcp-s030","q":"In a fan-out route, what is wrong with computing the number of batches this way?","a":"<p><code>Math.round</code> rounds down when the remainder is under half a batch, so the last recipients are never processed. For 10,040 emails and a batch of 100, the result is 100.4, which rounds to 100 batches, so the last 40 recipients are never processed. Use <code>Math.ceil</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>const batchSize = 100;\nconst numberOfBatches = Math.round(totalEmails / batchSize);</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"gcp-f0130","card":"q-gcp-032-emailing-subscribers-fan-out-pattern-exe","sec":"gcp-s030","q":"In a fan-out generator handler, what should happen if sending fails for some recipients?","a":"<p>Return a non-2xx status so Cloud Tasks retries the task, but make each send task idempotent so recipients already handled are not mailed again. Better still, have the generator create one send task per recipient, so a failure retries a single email, not the whole batch.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0131","card":"q-gcp-032-emailing-subscribers-fan-out-pattern-exe","sec":"gcp-s030","q":"Why should a task handler return a status quickly instead of doing everything inline?","a":"<p>Cloud Tasks treats a 2xx response as success and anything else, or a timeout, as failure to retry. A handler that works longer than the dispatch deadline gets retried while still running, which causes duplicate work. Keep each task small, and pass bigger work to further tasks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0132","card":"q-gcp-032-emailing-subscribers-fan-out-pattern-exe","sec":"gcp-s030","q":"A Node.js handler creates thousands of Cloud Tasks in a <code>for</code> loop, calling <code>await client.createTask(...)</code> once per iteration, so the calls run one at a time. How could you speed it up and what is the risk?","a":"<p>Create tasks concurrently with <code>Promise.all</code> over chunks of the list. This cuts the time spent in the generator handler. The risk is hitting API quotas or memory limits, so limit concurrency (for example in chunks of 50 to 100).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0133","card":"q-gcp-032-emailing-subscribers-fan-out-pattern-exe","sec":"gcp-s030","q":"In an Express app on App Engine where a user-facing route starts a bulk email fan-out through Cloud Tasks handlers, which HTTP methods would you use for the trigger route and for the task handlers, and why?","a":"<p>A browser-callable trigger can be GET, but anything that changes state is better as POST. Task handlers are POST because Cloud Tasks sends the payload in a POST body. Protect handlers from public calls (check the Cloud Tasks headers or require authentication) so nobody can trigger bulk sends.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0134","card":"q-gcp-033-8-cloud-scheduler-cron","sec":"gcp-s031","q":"What is Cloud Scheduler and when would you use it?","a":"<p>It is a fully managed cron service that triggers HTTP(S) endpoints, Pub/Sub topics or App Engine HTTP targets on a schedule. Use it for recurring work such as daily summary emails, nightly clean-ups or weekly backups, instead of running your own cron server. It is not a workflow engine; keep each job a short trigger.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0135","card":"q-gcp-033-8-cloud-scheduler-cron","sec":"gcp-s031","q":"What fields define a Cloud Scheduler job?","a":"<p>A name unique within the project and location, a description, a frequency in Unix cron format, a time zone and a target. You can also set a retry policy and an attempt deadline.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0136","card":"q-gcp-033-8-cloud-scheduler-cron","sec":"gcp-s031","q":"Cloud Scheduler can run your job twice or deliver late. How should the target be designed?","a":"<p>Make it idempotent. Cloud Scheduler gives at-least-once delivery, and retries can happen on failures. A job that sends a daily report should record that it already ran for that day, or put the real work behind Cloud Tasks so each unit can be deduplicated.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0137","card":"q-gcp-033-8-cloud-scheduler-cron","sec":"gcp-s031","q":"What is the difference between Cloud Scheduler and Cloud Tasks?","a":"<p>Cloud Scheduler fires recurring triggers on a time schedule. Cloud Tasks queues individual units of work that you create on demand, with rate limiting and retries. They combine well: a scheduler job calls an endpoint every hour, and that endpoint enqueues many tasks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0138","card":"q-gcp-034-cron-format","sec":"gcp-s032","q":"What are the five fields of the Unix cron expression used by Cloud Scheduler?","a":"<p>Minute (0-59), hour (0-23), day of month (1-31), month (1-12) and day of week (0-6, with Sunday as 0). Fields are separated by spaces, and <code>*</code> means every value.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0139","card":"q-gcp-034-cron-format","sec":"gcp-s032","q":"What do the cron expressions <code>0 8 * * *</code>, <code>0 * * * *</code> and <code>0 0 * * 0</code> mean?","a":"<p><code>0 8 * * *</code> runs daily at 08:00. <code>0 * * * *</code> runs at minute 0 of every hour. <code>0 0 * * 0</code> runs every Sunday at midnight. All use the job's time zone.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0140","card":"q-gcp-034-cron-format","sec":"gcp-s032","q":"What does the cron expression <code>*/15 9-17 * * 1-5</code> do?","a":"<p>It runs every 15 minutes (minutes 0, 15, 30, 45) during hours 9 through 17, on Monday to Friday. <code>*/n</code> means every n-th value, a dash is a range, and a comma gives a list.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0141","card":"q-gcp-034-cron-format","sec":"gcp-s032","q":"How would you write a cron expression to run at midnight on the first day of every month?","a":"<p><code>0 0 1 * *</code>. Minute 0, hour 0, day of month 1, any month, any day of week.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0142","card":"q-gcp-035-targets","sec":"gcp-s033","q":"What are the Cloud Scheduler target types and when do you use each?","a":"<p>HTTP calls any URL, including Cloud Run or a service in another project. Pub/Sub publishes a message to a topic, which suits fan-out to several subscribers. App Engine HTTP calls a relative path in an App Engine app of the same project, and you can pick service, version and instance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0143","card":"q-gcp-035-targets","sec":"gcp-s033","q":"Your Cloud Scheduler job must call an App Engine app that lives in a different project. Which target type do you use?","a":"<p>HTTP, with the full URL of the app. The App Engine HTTP target works only for the app in the current project. Secure the endpoint, for example with an OIDC token that Cloud Scheduler attaches to the request.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0144","card":"q-gcp-035-targets","sec":"gcp-s033","q":"How do you secure a Cloud Scheduler job that calls a private HTTP endpoint?","a":"<p>Configure an OIDC or OAuth token in the job, issued for a service account, and have the endpoint verify it. Requests from App Engine HTTP targets also carry a header <code>X-Appengine-Cron: true</code> that outside callers cannot set. Do not leave such an endpoint open to the internet without a check.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0145","card":"q-gcp-035-targets","sec":"gcp-s033","q":"How can you test a Cloud Scheduler job without waiting for its next scheduled time?","a":"<p>Run the job manually with Force run in the console or <code>gcloud scheduler jobs run &lt;job&gt;</code>. The result shows success or failure and the logs show what the target did.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0146","card":"q-gcp-036-timezone-caveats","sec":"gcp-s034","q":"Why does Cloud Scheduler recommend UTC, and what goes wrong with a local time zone?","a":"<p>Local time zones change with daylight saving time. A job set to a wall-clock time may run twice when clocks go back or be skipped when they go forward. UTC has no such shifts, so runs are predictable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0147","card":"q-gcp-036-timezone-caveats","sec":"gcp-s034","q":"You must email each user at 8 AM in their own time zone. How do you design this with Cloud Scheduler?","a":"<p>Run one job every hour in UTC (<code>0 * * * *</code>). The endpoint finds users whose local time is now 8 AM, using their stored time zone, and enqueues one Cloud Task per user. This avoids a job per time zone and works across DST changes if you compute local time with an IANA zone name such as <code>America/Los_Angeles</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0148","card":"q-gcp-036-timezone-caveats","sec":"gcp-s034","q":"Why store users' IANA time zone names (like <code>Europe/Paris</code>) instead of fixed UTC offsets or abbreviations?","a":"<p>The IANA names carry DST rules, so you can compute the correct local time at any date. Fixed offsets are wrong for half the year in DST regions, and abbreviations such as \"CST\" are ambiguous.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0149","card":"q-gcp-037-exercise-07-02-daily-subscriber-emails","sec":"gcp-s035","q":"An hourly Cloud Scheduler job triggers an App Engine endpoint that enqueues per-user email tasks. Why call it with POST and an empty body?","a":"<p>The endpoint reads everything it needs from the database and the current time, so no input is required. POST is used because it changes state (it enqueues work) and should not be triggered by prefetching or by a browser visit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0150","card":"q-gcp-037-exercise-07-02-daily-subscriber-emails","sec":"gcp-s035","q":"What is wrong with hard-coding the time zone in the query that selects users for the morning send?","a":"<p>The value is fixed, so only users in that one zone are chosen, and the name is a display name that changes with DST (Standard vs Daylight). The query should compute which zones are at the target hour right now, using IANA names, and select users in those zones.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>const timezone = &#x27;Pacific Daylight Time&#x27;;\nconst sql = &quot;SELECT email from emails WHERE timezone = ?&quot;;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"gcp-f0151","card":"q-gcp-038-9-operations-tools-logging-debugger-trac","sec":"gcp-s036","q":"Which Google Cloud tool would you use to read application logs, to inspect variables in a running app, and to find latency bottlenecks?","a":"<p>Cloud Logging for logs, Cloud Debugger for live variable inspection, and Cloud Trace for latency. Version note: Cloud Debugger was shut down in 2023, and Google points to the open-source Snapshot Debugger or local reproduction. Cloud Trace is still available, with OpenTelemetry the recommended way to instrument.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0152","card":"q-gcp-038-9-operations-tools-logging-debugger-trac","sec":"gcp-s036","q":"What are the three pillars of observability, and which Google Cloud tool covers each?","a":"<p>Logs (Cloud Logging), metrics (Cloud Monitoring) and traces (Cloud Trace). Error Reporting groups errors from logs. Together they let you detect a problem with metrics, find the failing request with a trace, and read the cause in the logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0153","card":"q-gcp-038-9-operations-tools-logging-debugger-trac","sec":"gcp-s036","q":"Why must tracing or debug agents such as <code>@google-cloud/trace-agent</code> be started before other <code>require</code> calls?","a":"<p>These agents patch modules like <code>http</code> and database drivers when they load, so they can record spans. A module loaded before the agent starts is not instrumented. Put the start call on the first line of the entry file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0154","card":"q-gcp-039-cloud-logging","sec":"gcp-s037","q":"How do logs from an App Engine Node.js app reach Cloud Logging?","a":"<p>Automatically. App Engine collects anything the app writes to stdout and stderr, so <code>console.log</code> works with no setup. Libraries like Winston or Bunyan also work, and structured JSON lines allow fields like severity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0155","card":"q-gcp-039-cloud-logging","sec":"gcp-s037","q":"How can logs from systems outside App Engine get into Cloud Logging?","a":"<p>Install the Logging (Ops) Agent on Compute Engine or AWS EC2 VMs, use a logging client library in your app, call the Logging REST API, or write with <code>gcloud logging write</code>. GKE, Cloud Run and Cloud Functions are collected automatically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0156","card":"q-gcp-039-cloud-logging","sec":"gcp-s037","q":"How do you write and read a log entry with the gcloud CLI, including a structured one?","a":"<p><code>gcloud logging write my-log \"A simple entry.\"</code> writes text. <code>gcloud logging write --payload-type=json my-log '{\"message\": \"hi\", \"weather\": \"cloudy\"}'</code> writes a structured entry. <code>gcloud logging read \"resource.type=global\"</code> reads entries that match a filter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0157","card":"q-gcp-039-cloud-logging","sec":"gcp-s037","q":"What is the benefit of structured (JSON) logs over plain text in Cloud Logging?","a":"<p>JSON fields become queryable and filterable (for example <code>jsonPayload.userId=\"42\"</code>), and special fields such as severity are recognized. That makes searching, alerting and log-based metrics more precise than parsing free text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0158","card":"q-gcp-039-cloud-logging","sec":"gcp-s037","q":"How do you keep logs longer than the default retention or analyze them in BigQuery?","a":"<p>Create a log sink in the Log Router that exports matching logs to Cloud Storage, BigQuery or Pub/Sub. Retention note: in current Cloud Logging the <code>_Default</code> bucket keeps logs 30 days unless you change it, and audit logs in <code>_Required</code> are kept 400 days. Check current documentation, since limits can change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0159","card":"q-gcp-039-cloud-logging","sec":"gcp-s037","q":"What are log-based metrics, and when are they useful?","a":"<p>They count log entries that match a filter (or extract a value from them) and publish the result to Cloud Monitoring. You can chart them and alert on them, for example alert when the count of <code>severity&gt;=ERROR</code> passes a threshold. They are useful when the code exposes no metric but writes a log line.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0160","card":"q-gcp-040-logs-viewer","sec":"gcp-s038","q":"In Logs Explorer (the Logs Viewer), how do you narrow thousands of entries to the ones you care about?","a":"<p>Use the query builder to filter by resource type, log name and severity, set a time range, then run the query. The histogram shows how often entries occur over time, and the field explorer counts values so you can click a value like an error severity to add it as a filter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0161","card":"q-gcp-040-logs-viewer","sec":"gcp-s038","q":"Your service started failing at some point. How do you use the Logs Viewer histogram to find the start?","a":"<p>Filter by severity ERROR and look for the first bar where volume jumps. Click it and use Jump to time to open entries around that moment, then expand entries to read the fields and stack traces. Compare that time to your deploy history.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0162","card":"q-gcp-040-logs-viewer","sec":"gcp-s038","q":"What query would show only error-level entries of App Engine apps in Logs Explorer?","a":"<p><code>resource.type=\"gae_app\" AND severity&gt;=ERROR</code>. The Logging query language combines field comparisons with AND, OR and NOT, and supports <code>&gt;=</code> on severity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0163","card":"q-gcp-041-cloud-debugger-exercise-08-03","sec":"gcp-s039","q":"What did Cloud Debugger do for a production app on GCP, and what is its current status?","a":"<p>It captured snapshots (call stack and variables) and logpoints from live traffic without stopping the app, which helps with bugs that are hard to reproduce locally. Version note: Google Cloud shut Cloud Debugger down in May 2023. Alternatives are the open-source Snapshot Debugger, better structured logs and traces, or reproducing locally.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0164","card":"q-gcp-041-cloud-debugger-exercise-08-03","sec":"gcp-s039","q":"What was the difference between a snapshot and a logpoint in Cloud Debugger?","a":"<p>A snapshot is taken once at a line on the next matching request and records the call stack and variable values. A logpoint adds a log message at a line, with <code>{expression}</code> placeholders, for every matching hit, without changing the source or redeploying. Snapshots show state; logpoints stream values to the logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0165","card":"q-gcp-041-cloud-debugger-exercise-08-03","sec":"gcp-s039","q":"How do you make a Cloud Debugger snapshot fire only for a particular iteration of a loop?","a":"<p>Add a condition on the snapshot, such as <code>i === 1</code> for a loop counter <code>i</code>, so it fires only when the expression is true. Other expressions, such as an array element <code>items[i]</code>, can also be evaluated and recorded. Version note: this applies to the retired Cloud Debugger, and similar ideas exist in other debuggers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0166","card":"q-gcp-042-cloud-trace-exercise-08-04","sec":"gcp-s040","q":"What is a trace, and what is a span in Cloud Trace?","a":"<p>A trace is the record of one request as it moves through your system. A span is one timed step inside it, such as a database call or an outgoing HTTP request. Spans are nested and show which step took the longest, so you can find the bottleneck.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0167","card":"q-gcp-042-cloud-trace-exercise-08-04","sec":"gcp-s040","q":"Why does Cloud Trace sample only a subset of requests, and what does that mean for debugging?","a":"<p>Recording every request would add overhead and cost. Sampling keeps the load low and still shows the latency distribution. A specific rare request may not have a trace, so for a single failing request rely on logs or raise the sample rate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0168","card":"q-gcp-042-cloud-trace-exercise-08-04","sec":"gcp-s040","q":"A request endpoint in your Node.js App Engine app is slow. How do you find out why with Cloud Trace?","a":"<p>Start the trace agent as the first line (<code>require('@google-cloud/trace-agent').start()</code>), deploy, send traffic, then open the trace list and filter by minimum latency. Open a slow trace, and the span with the largest duration points to the cause, such as a sequential outgoing call or a slow query. Then link to the matching log entry for more detail.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0169","card":"q-gcp-042-cloud-trace-exercise-08-04","sec":"gcp-s040","q":"Is Cloud Trace free?","a":"<p>Pricing note: a free allowance of spans per month applies (2.5 million at the time of writing) and the rest is charged per span. Check current pricing. Lower the sampling rate to reduce cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"gcp-f0170","card":"q-gcp-042-cloud-trace-exercise-08-04","sec":"gcp-s040","q":"Why does tracing code with several <code>await sleep(...)</code> and <code>axios.get(...)</code> calls in a loop show up as a slow, sequential pattern?","a":"<p>Each <code>await</code> waits for the previous call to finish, so the spans appear one after another and the request time is the sum. If the calls are independent, run them in parallel with <code>Promise.all</code> and the total drops to about the slowest one.</p>","hand":true}];
